QUESTIONS = [
    # CH51 Finance Commission L1 (+28)
    {"text":"Under which Article of the Constitution is the Finance Commission constituted?","options":["Article 268","Article 275","Article 280","Article 315"],"correct_answer":"Article 280","explanation":"Article 280 of the Constitution provides for a Finance Commission as a quasi-judicial body to recommend the distribution of financial resources between the Union and the States.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"Who constitutes the Finance Commission in India?","options":["The Parliament","The Prime Minister","The President of India","The Union Finance Minister"],"correct_answer":"The President of India","explanation":"The Finance Commission is constituted by the President of India every fifth year or at such earlier time as he considers necessary.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"How frequently is the Finance Commission typically constituted?","options":["Every 3 years","Every 4 years","Every 5 years","Every 6 years"],"correct_answer":"Every 5 years","explanation":"According to the Constitution, the Finance Commission is constituted every fifth year, though the President can constitute it earlier if necessary.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"What is the composition of the Finance Commission?","options":["A Chairman and two other members","A Chairman and three other members","A Chairman and four other members","A Chairman and five other members"],"correct_answer":"A Chairman and four other members","explanation":"The Finance Commission consists of a Chairman and four other members, all appointed by the President.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"Who specifies the qualifications necessary for appointment as members of the Finance Commission?","options":["The President","The Parliament","The Supreme Court","The Union Cabinet"],"correct_answer":"The Parliament","explanation":"The Constitution authorizes the Parliament to determine the qualifications of members of the Commission and the manner in which they should be selected (done via The Finance Commission (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act, 1951).","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which of the following is an essential qualification for the Chairman of the Finance Commission?","options":["A retired judge of the Supreme Court","A person with experience in public affairs","A former Governor of a state","An expert in international trade"],"correct_answer":"A person with experience in public affairs","explanation":"According to the qualifications prescribed by Parliament, the Chairman of the Finance Commission should be a person having experience in public affairs.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"Are the members of the Finance Commission eligible for reappointment?","options":["Yes, they are eligible for reappointment","No, the Constitution bars their reappointment","Yes, but only for a maximum of 2 years","Yes, but only as Chairman"],"correct_answer":"Yes, they are eligible for reappointment","explanation":"Unlike the UPSC members or the CAG, the members of the Finance Commission hold office for such period as specified by the President in his order and ARE eligible for reappointment.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which of the following is the core function of the Finance Commission?","options":["Preparing the annual Union Budget","Allocating funds for Five Year Plans","Recommending the distribution of net proceeds of taxes between Union and States","Auditing the accounts of the Union Government"],"correct_answer":"Recommending the distribution of net proceeds of taxes between Union and States","explanation":"Its primary duty is to make recommendations to the President as to the distribution of net proceeds of divisible taxes between the Union and States, and their allocation among the states.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"The Finance Commission recommends the principles that should govern which types of grants to the states?","options":["Discretionary grants under Article 282","Statutory tied grants only","Grants-in-aid of the revenues of the states out of the Consolidated Fund of India","Plan grants given by the NITI Aayog"],"correct_answer":"Grants-in-aid of the revenues of the states out of the Consolidated Fund of India","explanation":"The Finance Commission recommends the principles governing grants-in-aid to the states out of the Consolidated Fund of India (statutory grants under Article 275).","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"Following the 73rd and 74th Amendments, what new function was added to the Union Finance Commission?","options":["To directly fund local bodies","To suggest measures to augment the State Consolidated Fund to supplement the resources of panchayats and municipalities","To audit the accounts of local bodies","To delimit the territorial constituencies of panchayats"],"correct_answer":"To suggest measures to augment the State Consolidated Fund to supplement the resources of panchayats and municipalities","explanation":"The Finance Commission must now recommend measures to augment the Consolidated Fund of a State to supplement the resources of the Panchayats and Municipalities on the basis of the recommendations made by the State Finance Commission.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"From which fund are the Grants-in-aid recommended by the Finance Commission given to the states?","options":["Contingency Fund of India","Consolidated Fund of India","Public Account of India","Prime Minister's Relief Fund"],"correct_answer":"Consolidated Fund of India","explanation":"The grants-in-aid recommended by the Finance Commission are dispersed from the Consolidated Fund of India to states that are in need of assistance.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"Who was the Chairman of the First Finance Commission of India?","options":["K.C. Neogy","K. Santhanam","A.K. Chanda","P.V. Rajamannar"],"correct_answer":"K.C. Neogy","explanation":"The First Finance Commission was constituted in 1951, and its Chairman was Mr. K.C. Neogy.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"What is the nature of the recommendations made by the Finance Commission?","options":["Binding on the Government of India","Binding on the State Governments only","Advisory in nature","Judicial and legally enforceable orders"],"correct_answer":"Advisory in nature","explanation":"The recommendations of the Finance Commission are only of advisory nature and hence, not binding on the government. It is up to the Union government to implement its recommendations.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"Before which body does the President lay the report of the Finance Commission?","options":["The Supreme Court","The State Legislatures","Each House of Parliament","The NITI Aayog"],"correct_answer":"Each House of Parliament","explanation":"Under Article 281, the President shall cause every recommendation made by the Finance Commission along with an explanatory memorandum as to the action taken thereon, to be laid before each House of Parliament.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which of the following is NOT an essential qualification for the four members of the Finance Commission according to the 1951 Act?","options":["A person qualified to be appointed as a Judge of a High Court","A person having special knowledge of the finances of the Government","A person experienced in financial matters and administration","A retired Chief Election Commissioner"],"correct_answer":"A retired Chief Election Commissioner","explanation":"The four members should be: 1. A HC judge or one qualified to be appointed as one 2. A person with special knowledge of finance/accounts of govt 3. A person with wide experience in financial matters/administration 4. A person with special knowledge of economics. There's no requirement to be an ex-CEC.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which body was often considered a competing body to the Finance Commission in calculating planning grants until recent years?","options":["The Reserve Bank of India","The Planning Commission","The Election Commission","The Comptroller and Auditor General"],"correct_answer":"The Planning Commission","explanation":"Historically, the Planning Commission provided plan grants (discretionary) which often overshadowed the Finance Commission's non-plan statutory grants, leading to overlapping roles.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"Can the President refer any other matter to the Finance Commission in the interest of sound finance?","options":["No, its functions are strictly limited to Article 280(3)(a) and (b)","Yes, under Article 280(3)(d)","Only if Parliament passes a specific resolution","Only during a Financial Emergency"],"correct_answer":"Yes, under Article 280(3)(d)","explanation":"Article 280(3) states that it shall be the duty of the Commission to make recommendations to the President on any other matter referred to the Commission by the President in the interests of sound finance.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"The Constitution characterises the Finance Commission as the 'balancing wheel' of:","options":["Parliamentary Democracy","Fiscal Federalism in India","Cooperative Federalism","The Judicial System"],"correct_answer":"Fiscal Federalism in India","explanation":"The Finance Commission is envisaged in the Constitution as the balancing wheel of fiscal federalism in India, ensuring states don't starve for resources given their limited taxation powers relative to their expenditure responsibilities.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"To whom does the State Finance Commission submit its recommendations?","options":["The President","The Union Finance Commission","The Governor of the State","The Chief Minister"],"correct_answer":"The Governor of the State","explanation":"Just as the Union Finance Commission submits its report to the President, the State Finance Commission submits its report to the Governor of the respective state.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which Constitutional Article deals with the State Finance Commission extending funds to Panchayats?","options":["Article 243-I","Article 243-K","Article 243-ZD","Article 280"],"correct_answer":"Article 243-I","explanation":"Article 243-I deals with the constitution of the State Finance Commission to review the financial position of the Panchayats and recommend principles governing distribution of taxes between State and Panchayats.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which body specifies the terms of reference (ToR) for a newly constituted Finance Commission?","options":["The Parliament","The Prime Minister's Office","The President of India on the advice of the Union Cabinet","The Chairman of the Commission"],"correct_answer":"The President of India on the advice of the Union Cabinet","explanation":"The Terms of Reference (ToR) for each Finance Commission are defined by the President of India (acting on the aid and advice of the Union Cabinet) in the order constituting the commission.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"The division of tax proceeds between the Union and the States is known as:","options":["Horizontal Devolution","Vertical Devolution","Lateral Devolution","Parallel Devolution"],"correct_answer":"Vertical Devolution","explanation":"Vertical devolution refers to the share of net proceeds of union taxes that goes to the states collectively. Horizontal devolution is the distribution of that share among the individual states.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"The distribution of the states' share of taxes among the various states themselves is known as:","options":["Vertical Devolution","Diagonal Devolution","Horizontal Devolution","Equitable Distribution Formula"],"correct_answer":"Horizontal Devolution","explanation":"Horizontal devolution refers to the formula/criteria used by the Finance Commission to distribute the total pool of state revenues among individual states (e.g., based on population, area, income distance).","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"What is an essential component of the explanatory memorandum presented with the Finance Commission report?","options":["The GDP growth projections","The action taken by the government on the recommendations","A list of dissenting notes by states","The global economic outlook"],"correct_answer":"The action taken by the government on the recommendations","explanation":"Under Article 281, the explanatory memorandum must detail the action taken by the Government on the recommendations of the Finance Commission.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"What happens to the remaining tax proceeds that are not distributed to the states?","options":["They are given to local bodies directly","They are returned to the taxpayers","They are retained by the Union Government","They are kept in the National Investment Fund"],"correct_answer":"They are retained by the Union Government","explanation":"The divisible pool of central taxes is divided between the Centre and the States. The portion not devolved to the states is retained by the Union Government to meet its own expenditure responsibilities.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"Are Cesses and Surcharges levied by the Union Government normally part of the divisible pool of taxes?","options":["Yes, always","No, they are exclusively retained by the Union Government","Yes, provided they exceed a certain amount","They are entirely given to the states"],"correct_answer":"No, they are exclusively retained by the Union Government","explanation":"Under the Constitution, cesses (for specific purposes) and surcharges on taxes (under Article 271) do not form part of the divisible pool and are exclusively available to the Centre.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"Has the Constitution limited the Finance Commission to dealing only with income tax?","options":["Yes","No, it deals with all net proceeds of divisible taxes","Yes, but states can opt for others","Only income tax and corporation tax"],"correct_answer":"No, it deals with all net proceeds of divisible taxes","explanation":"Since the 80th Amendment (2000), an alternative scheme of devolution was introduced wherein all central taxes and duties (except certain surcharges/cesses) form the divisible pool, not just income tax.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},
    {"text":"Which Article requires the Government to lay the Explanatory Memorandum to the FC report before Parliament?","options":["Article 280","Article 281","Article 282","Article 112"],"correct_answer":"Article 281","explanation":"Article 281 (Recommendations of the Finance Commission) dictates that the President shall lay the recommendations together with an explanatory memorandum of action taken before each House of Parliament.","chapter_number":51,"level":1},

    # CH51 Finance Commission L2 (+23)
    {"text":"Consider the following regarding the Finance Commission:\n1. The Constitution clearly specifies the qualifications necessary for appointment as members of the Commission.\n2. The Constitution empowers Parliament to determine the qualifications of its members by law.\n3. The members are appointed for a fixed 5-year tenure that cannot be altered by the President.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect: The Constitution leaves it to Parliament (Article 280(2)). Statement 2 is correct. Statement 3 is incorrect: Members hold office for such period as specified by the President in his order, not a strict 5 years.","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"Which of the following are the required qualifications for members of the Finance Commission as per the 1951 Act?\n1. A person qualified to be appointed as a Judge of a High Court.\n2. A person with special knowledge of the finances and accounts of the government.\n3. A person with special knowledge of economics.\n4. A person who has been a Chief Minister of a state.\nSelect the correct answer:","options":["1, 2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2, 3 and 4"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3 only","explanation":"The Act lists four specific qualifications for the four members: HC judge qual, special knowledge of govt finance/accounts, wide experience in financial matters/admin, and special knowledge of economics. Being a former CM is not a statutory qualification.","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"Regarding the binding nature of the Finance Commission:\n1. It is mandatory for the Government of India to accept its recommendations regarding tax devolution.\n2. The Supreme Court has ruled that if the Union Government rejects the advice of the FC, it must provide reasons to the Parliament.\n3. The recommendations are purely advisory and the Government can choose not to implement them.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect; the recommendations are advisory, not legally binding. However, by constitutional convention and Article 281 (memo of action taken), the government accepts them and must explain any non-acceptance to Parliament.","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the difference between Article 275 and Article 282 grants:\n1. Article 275 deals with statutory grants recommended by the Finance Commission.\n2. Article 282 deals with discretionary grants made by the Union or State on matters which may not be within their legislative competence.\n3. Both types of grants are completely determined by the Finance Commission.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. The Finance Commission only recommends statutory grants under Article 275. Discretionary grants under Article 282 were historically managed by the Planning Commission / Union ministries.","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"What changes did the 73rd and 74th Amendments bring to the functions of the Finance Commission?\n1. It must decide the formula for sharing state taxes with local bodies directly.\n2. It must suggest measures to augment the State Consolidated Fund to assist Panchayats and Municipalities.\n3. Its recommendations regarding local bodies must be based on the reports of the State Finance Commissions.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect because the State Finance Commission (not the Union FC) decides the sharing formula between the state and its local bodies. The Union FC only suggests measures to augment the state's fund based on the SFC's report (Statements 2 and 3).","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the powers of the Finance Commission as a quasi-judicial body:\n1. It has all the powers of a civil court under the Code of Civil Procedure in respect of summoning witnesses.\n2. It can require any person to furnish information on such points or matters as in the opinion of the Commission may be useful.\n3. It can punish individuals for contempt of court if they fail to appear.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect; while it has powers of a civil court for summoning, producing documents, etc., it does not possess the constitutional power to punish for contempt of court, which is reserved for HC/SC.","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"The 'horizontal devolution' formula designed by a Finance Commission typically includes which parameters?\n1. Population\n2. Income Distance (or Fiscal Capacity distance)\n3. Forest and Ecology\n4. Number of political representatives in Parliament\nSelect the correct answer:","options":["1, 2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2, 3 and 4 only","1, 2, 3 and 4"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3 only","explanation":"Political representation is never a criterion. The FC uses objective criteria like Population (usually 1971 or 2011), Area, Income distance (poorer states get more), Forest cover, and Demographic performance.","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the relationship between the Finance Commission and the erstwhile Planning Commission:\n1. The Finance Commission is a constitutional body while the Planning Commission was an extra-constitutional executive body.\n2. The Constitution originally envisioned both bodies to allocate funds to states.\n3. The existence of the Planning Commission often restricted the scope of the Finance Commission to just non-plan revenue expenditures.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect; the Constitution never envisioned the Planning Commission (it was created by an executive resolution). Statement 3 accurately describes how plan-grants dominated, restricting the FC's role until the Planning Commission was abolished.","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"Which of the following bodies play a role in India's fiscal federalism architecture?\n1. GST Council\n2. Finance Commission\n3. Inter-State Council\nSelect the correct answer:","options":["1 and 2 only","2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All three play roles: GST Council handles indirect tax base/rates (Art 279A). Finance Commission handles revenue distribution (Art 280). The Inter-State Council discusses matters of common interest, which includes fiscal coordination and federal relations.","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"Regarding the disqualification of a member of the Finance Commission, under the 1951 Act, they shall be disqualified if:\n1. They are of unsound mind.\n2. They are an undischarged insolvent.\n3. They have been convicted of an offence involving moral turpitude.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"The Finance Commission (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act, 1951 specifies that a person shall be disqualified if they are of unsound mind, an undischarged insolvent, convicted of an offence involving moral turpitude, or have financial interests that prejudicially affect their functions.","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the 'terms of reference' (ToR) of the Finance Commission:\n1. The ToR is rigid and cannot include anything beyond tax devolution and grants-in-aid.\n2. The President frequently includes specific additional matters in the ToR in the 'interests of sound finance'.\n3. States have the constitutional right to vote on the ToR before the Commission is formed.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Statements 1 is incorrect because Article 280(3)(d) allows 'any other matter' to be referred. Statement 3 is incorrect; the ToR is formulated by the Union Government (President) without a constitutional mandate requiring state votes, though states often demand consultation.","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"What is the impact of placing cess and surcharges outside the divisible pool?\n1. It increases the total funds shared automatically with the states.\n2. It allows the Union Government to raise revenues exclusively for its own use or specific Central schemes.\n3. It has led to complaints from states about the shrinking of the actual divisible pool.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect; it DECREASES the funds shared with the states because cesses/surcharges bypass the FC's devolution formula. Hence, states frequently complain (Statement 3) that the Centre relies heavily on cesses (Statement 2).","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the difference between statutory grants and discretionary grants:\n1. Statutory grants are charged on the Consolidated Fund of India.\n2. Discretionary grants are given primarily bypassing the Finance Commission under Article 282.\n3. Statutory grants are only available for plan expenditure, not for non-plan expenditure.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect; statutory grants (Art 275) recommended by the FC are traditionally primarily for non-plan revenue deficits or specific purposes, not just plan expenditure. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"Regarding the State Finance Commission (SFC):\n1. It is constituted every five years by the Governor of the state.\n2. It recommends the principles for distribution of taxes between the state and the municipalities.\n3. It determines the taxes, duties, and tolls that may be assigned to the Panchayats.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All statements are correct. The SFC (Arts 243-I and 243-Y) constitutes the core of fiscal decentralization within a state, handling distributions to both Panchayats and Municipalities, and assigning specific taxes to them.","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"How does the introduction of GST impact the functioning of the Finance Commission?\n1. It removed the Finance Commission's power to recommend devolution of direct taxes.\n2. The FC must now consider the revenue projections of states post-GST implementation and any compensation cess mechanism.\n3. GST Council decisions directly affect the size of the divisible pool of indirect taxes that the FC distributes.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect; GST only subsumed indirect taxes. Direct taxes (Income tax, corporate tax) remain in the divisible pool. Statements 2 and 3 are correct, as GST fundamentally altered indirect tax collections, impacting FC calculations.","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the 'Income Distance' criterion used by Finance Commissions:\n1. It is formulated to provide higher tax shares to states with lower per capita income.\n2. It measures the distance of a state's per capita income from the state with the highest per capita income.\n3. It promotes equity among states in the horizontal devolution formula.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All are correct. 'Income Distance' is a core equity parameter. It ensures that poorer states receive a greater share of the central tax pool to help them provide a standard level of public services, ensuring horizontal equity.","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"If a state faces a post-devolution revenue deficit, what mechanism does the Finance Commission use to help the state?\n1. It increases the state's horizontal share of taxes.\n2. It recommends a specific Revenue Deficit Grant under Article 275.\n3. It permits the state to borrow directly from the Reserve Bank of India without limits.\nWhich is correct?","options":["1 only","2 only","3 only","1 and 2 only"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"If a state's projected revenue remains in a deficit even after the share of central taxes (tax devolution) is transferred, the FC explicitly recommends Post-Devolution Revenue Deficit Grants under Article 275. (It doesn't alter the objective horizontal formula just to plug a specific deficit).","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the composition of the divisible pool of taxes:\n1. Corporation Tax\n2. Income Tax\n3. Export Duties\n4. Goods and Services Tax (CGST and IGST portion)\nWhich of these are generally included in the divisible pool recommended by the FC?","options":["1, 2 and 4 only","1, 2 and 3 only","2, 3 and 4 only","1, 2, 3 and 4"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 4 only","explanation":"Export Duties (and customs duties historically depending on amendments, but strictly export duties are mostly negligible/exempt or not shared the same way) and specifically cesses/surcharges aren't shared. However, since the 80th Amendment, all Central taxes (Corporation, Income, CGST) form the divisible pool except cesses, surcharges, and costs of collection. (Export duties are barely levied now, and typically don't form the core, but CGST/Income/Corp are the massive pillars).","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"Regarding the constitutional convention of the FC recommendations:\n1. The government must introduce a constitutional amendment to implement the horizontal devolution formula.\n2. The government enforces tax devolution by an executive order based on the FC report.\n3. Parliament votes on and passes the FC recommendations as a money bill to make them valid.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 only","1 and 2 only","3 only","2 and 3 only"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"The recommendations of the FC regarding tax sharing are implemented via an executive order of the President. Grants-in-aid (Article 275) find provision in the Union budget. It doesn't require a constitutional amendment or a specific FC money bill.","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the relationship between the NITI Aayog and the Finance Commission:\n1. Both bodies have the constitutional power to mandate tax devolution to states.\n2. NITI Aayog replaced the Planning Commission but does not allocate financial resources/grants to states like the Planning Commission did.\n3. Because NITI Aayog doesn't allocate funds, the relative importance of the Finance Commission in fiscal federalism has increased.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect; NITI Aayog is not a constitutional body and has no power to mandate tax devolution. Statements 2 and 3 are correct. The shift from PC to NITI Aayog ended the era of discretionary plan grants, making the FC the undisputed primary mechanism for resource transfer.","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"What is 'Demographic Performance' as introduced in recent Finance Commission criteria?\n1. A reward for states that successfully controlled their population growth.\n2. A penalty for states with a demographic dividend of young workers.\n3. It was introduced to offset the disadvantage to southern states caused by shifting the population base year from 1971 to 2011.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Recent FCs (like 15th) used 2011 population data but introduced 'Demographic Performance' as a criterion to deliberately reward states with lower fertility rates (mostly southern states), mitigating their loss in tax share due to their successful population control compared to 1971 levels.","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"The provision protecting the salaries of SPSC members from being varied to their disadvantage does NOT apply to Finance Commission members. Why?\n1. FC members are temporary (constituted every 5 years), not permanent service members like SPSC.\n2. The Constitution explicitly states FC members serve pro bono without salary.\n3. FC members are strictly drawn from the Union Cabinet.\nWhich is correct?","options":["1 only","2 only","3 only","1 and 2 only"],"correct_answer":"1 only","explanation":"The Finance Commission is an ad-hoc body appointed every 5 years (Statement 1). They do receive salaries (determined by rules), but there is no specific constitutional proviso preventing variation to their disadvantage post-appointment like there is for UPSC/SPSC/Supreme Court judges, because it's not a standing continuing tribunal.","chapter_number":51,"level":2},
    {"text":"Consider the 'Area' criterion in horizontal devolution:\n1. It recognizes that delivering public services in a larger state requires higher administrative costs.\n2. It generally includes a capping mechanism so that very large states do not take an overwhelmingly massive share.\n3. It is given the highest weightage, superseding population and income distance.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. 'Income Distance' and 'Population' usually carry the highest weightage (e.g., 45% and 15%). 'Area' is usually around 15%, and it often has a floor/cap to prevent extreme skewing. Statements 1 and 2 correctly reflect its rationale.","chapter_number":51,"level":2},

    # CH51 Finance Commission L3 (+23)
    {"text":"Consider the historical evolution of the divisible pool vis-a-vis the 80th Constitutional Amendment Act, 2000:\n1. Prior to this, only Income Tax (Art 270) and Union Excise Duties (Art 272) were shared with states.\n2. The amendment was enacted based on the recommendations of the Tenth Finance Commission.\n3. It introduced the 'Alternative Scheme of Devolution' creating a single divisible pool of all Central taxes.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All statements are correct. The 10th FC (under K.C. Pant) recommended pooling all central taxes (Corporate tax, Customs, etc.) instead of just Income tax and Excise. This was given effect via the 80th Amendment Act, 2000, creating a robust single divisible pool.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"Regarding the constitutional mandate of the Finance Commission under Article 280(3)(c):\n1. It requires the FC to suggest measures needed to augment the Consolidated Fund of a State to supplement resources of Panchayats/Municipalities.\n2. The FC must directly allocate funds from the CF of India to the accounts of individual village panchayats.\n3. This provision was originally part of the 1950 Constitution.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect; funds go to the State, which disburses them to local bodies. Statement 3 is incorrect; this sub-clause was added by the 73rd and 74th Amendments (1992). Thus, only Statement 1 is correct.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the tension surrounding 'Terms of Reference' (ToR) of the Finance Commission:\n1. Article 280 allows the President to dictate any specific policy parameters (like assessing populist welfare schemes) for the FC to consider.\n2. States often argue that restrictive/conditional ToRs violate the intended constitutional independence of the FC's devolution formula.\n3. The Supreme Court has the power to strike down a ToR if it is politically biased against non-central ruling parties.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect; the ToR formulation is an executive/legislative prerogative of the Union and the SC generally does not interfere in such fiscal policy directives. Statements 1 and 2 are true; the Union often includes parameters via Article 280(3)(d), which states contest.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"Evaluate the 'Grants-in-aid' powers in the Constitution:\n1. Article 275 grants are statutory, given out of the Consolidated Fund of India, based on FC recommendations.\n2. Article 282 grants are discretionary, meaning the Union can give them for public purposes outside its legislative competence.\n3. The Gadgil-Mukherjee formula was historically used to distribute Article 275 FC grants.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. The Gadgil-Mukherjee formula was devised by the National Development Council (NDC) to allocate Planning Commission (Article 282) plan grants, NOT Finance Commission (Article 275) grants.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"In the context of fiscal federalism, what does the term 'Vertical Imbalance' refer to, and how does the Constitution address it?\n1. It refers to the mismatch between the revenue-raising capacity of states and their expenditure responsibilities.\n2. The Constitution assigns elastic and buoyant taxes (like Income tax/Corp tax) mostly to the Union, creating this imbalance.\n3. The Finance Commission's 'Vertical Devolution' mandate is the primary constitutional mechanism to correct this imbalance.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1, 2 and 3","explanation":"All statements are correct. Vertical imbalance is inherent because the Centre commands major tax bases while states handle heavy expenditures (police, health, education). The FC resolves this by mandating a transfer (e.g., 41% or 42%) from the Centre to the States collectively.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"Examine the 'Forest and Ecology' criterion used by recent Finance Commissions:\n1. It rewards states for maintaining dense forest covers, compensating them for the loss of economic activity and revenue.\n2. It acts as an ecological fiscal transfer, promoting environmental conservation.\n3. It only applies to the Himalayan and North-Eastern states.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. The 'Forest and Ecology' criterion (usually weighted at 7.5% - 10%) applies to all states based on their share of dense forest cover, not just Himalayan/NE states. Statements 1 and 2 accurately describe its purpose.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the difference between Surcharges and Cesses in the context of the divisible pool:\n1. Under Article 271, Parliament can levy surcharges on certain taxes, which entirely accrue to the Union.\n2. A cess is levied for a specific purpose (e.g., Education Cess) and is also kept out of the divisible pool.\n3. The Finance Commission has the constitutional authority to bring surcharges into the divisible pool if it deems fit.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. The exclusion of surcharges from the divisible pool is explicitly mandated by the Constitution (Article 271 read with Article 270). The FC cannot override the Constitution to include them. Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"With respect to the 15th Finance Commission's recommendations for Jammu & Kashmir:\n1. J&K was treated as a State for the horizontal devolution of taxes.\n2. The vertical devolution was reduced from 42% (in 14th FC) to 41% to account for the creation of UTs of J&K and Ladakh.\n3. The Centre assumed the expenditure responsibilities of the new UTs, hence the 1% adjustment.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect. As J&K became a Union Territory, it no longer receives a share from the 'state' divisible pool (devolution). Instead, its financial needs are met directly by the Union budget. Hence, the FC reduced the state share from 42% to 41% (Statements 2 and 3).","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"Analyse the constitutional significance of Article 281:\n1. It forces transparency on the executive branch regarding financial distribution.\n2. If the Government modifies the FC's recommendations, it must state the reasons for such modification in the explanatory memorandum.\n3. It renders the FC's advice legally binding upon the Parliament.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect because Article 281 does not make the advice legally binding. It only forces the government to lay the report and the action taken (Explanatory Memorandum) before Parliament, ensuring political and parliamentary accountability (Statements 1 and 2).","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the 'State Disaster Response Fund' (SDRF) and 'National Disaster Response Fund' (NDRF) with respect to the Finance Commission:\n1. The Finance Commission recommends the corpus and the sharing pattern of the SDRF between the Centre and States.\n2. The 15th Finance Commission recommended a new overarching 'National Disaster Risk Management Fund'.\n3. Disaster management funding is strictly outside the purview of the FC and is handled solely by the Home Ministry.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. The Finance Commission is explicitly tasked with reviewing the arrangements for financing disaster management. It determines the allocations for SDRF and NDRF. Statements 1 and 2 are correct reflections of recent FC tasks.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"What is the 'Fiscal Deficit' and 'Debt' conditionalities often imposed by the Finance Commission?\n1. The FC often recommends a fiscal consolidation roadmap prescribing FRBM limits for states.\n2. The FC can recommend tying certain grant releases to the states achieving specific ease-of-doing-business or power-sector reforms.\n3. The Supreme Court has prohibited the FC from attaching conditions to statutory grants under Article 275.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. The FC frequently recommends performance-based or reform-linked grants (e.g., power sector reforms, municipal tax collections), and this is perfectly constitutional. Statements 1 and 2 accurately describe how the FC guides macro-fiscal stability.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"Compare the Finance Commission with the Goods and Services Tax (GST) Council:\n1. Both derive their existence from the Constitution (Articles 280 and 279A respectively).\n2. The FC decides how tax revenues are shared, while the GST Council decides the base and rates of indirect taxes.\n3. Decisions in the FC are taken by a 3/4th majority vote of its members including state representatives.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. The FC is an expert body of 5 members who arrive at recommendations typically by consensus or majority of the experts; it does not include voting representatives from states. The GST Council is the federal body that requires a 3/4th weighted majority vote.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the 'Tax Effort' criterion used in some Finance Commissions:\n1. It rewards states whose Own Tax Revenue (OTR) to GSDP ratio is high or improving.\n2. It addresses the criticism that high tax devolution might create a perverse incentive for states to collect less of their own taxes.\n3. It is universally accepted as the criterion with the highest weightage to promote efficiency.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. 'Tax Effort' or 'Tax capacity' is usually given a very low weightage (e.g., 2.5%) compared to equity criteria like Income Distance (45%) and Population (15%). Statements 1 and 2 accurately describe its purpose to avoid the 'lazy state' syndrome.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"Regarding the 'Post-Devolution Revenue Deficit' (PDRD) grants:\n1. These grants are temporary and usually tapered down over the FC's tenure.\n2. They are meant to bridge the gap between a state's assessed revenue receipts and its assessed non-plan/revenue expenditure.\n3. They are provided as discretionary grants under Article 282.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. PDRD grants are statutory grants provided under Article 275 based on the FC's recommendations. Statements 1 and 2 are true; the FC assesses revenues and expenditures, and if a gap remains after tax devolution, a PDRD grant is assigned, often tapering down to force fiscal discipline.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"How does the Finance Commission deal with the issue of 'Populism' built into the Terms of Reference (ToR)?\n1. It performs a normative assessment of state expenditures to strip away unsustainable populist freebies.\n2. It cannot legally question how states spend their money due to the federal structure.\n3. Recent ToRs have explicitly asked the FC to propose measurable performance-based incentives to check populist spending.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is incorrect. The FC does question spending; it uses a 'normative approach' to assess state expenditures, essentially assuming a standard level of efficiency and discounting unsustainable or excessive 'populist' expenditures when calculating revenue deficits. Statements 1 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"Analyse the constitutional protection of states' share of taxes (Article 274):\n1. No bill which varies any tax in which states are interested can be introduced in Parliament without the prior recommendation of the President.\n2. This acts as a safeguard preventing the Union from unilaterally shrinking the divisible pool without presidential scrutiny.\n3. State Legislatures must ratify any bill altering the income tax structure.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. Altering income tax structures (rates, slabs) in the Finance Bill does not require state legislature ratification (unlike constitutional amendments). Statements 1 and 2 correctly describe the protection afforded by Article 274 requiring prior Presidential recommendation.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"What is the significance of the 13th Finance Commission introducing the concept of a 'Grand Bargain' for GST?\n1. It suggested a compensation package for states to agree to surrender their indirect tax autonomy to the GST regime.\n2. It recommended that the FC calculate state-wise compensation based on an assumed 14% growth rate.\n3. It mandated that a permanent GST body replace the Finance Commission entirely.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 only","explanation":"Statement 2 refers to the actual GST Compensation Act mechanism, not the specific 13th FC recommendation (which proposed a 50,000 cr fund and a model GST). Statement 3 is false. Statement 1 is correct; the 13th FC laid the intellectual groundwork for compensating states to induce them to adopt GST.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"Evaluate the 15th FC's Sector-specific and State-specific grants:\n1. They are statutory grants under Art 275 targeted at health, education, and judiciary.\n2. The Union Government has generally accepted these in totality to ensure targeted development.\n3. They attempt to ensure absolute equality of development across all districts in India.\nWhich is correct?","options":["1 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 only","explanation":"Statement 2 is historically incorrect currently; the Union Government accepted the horizontal/vertical devolution but specifically kept the sector/state-specific grants of the 15th FC 'under consideration' and largely did not devolve them separately. Statement 3 is an absolute impossible extreme. Statement 1 is correct in identifying what they are.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the 'Cess and Surcharge' debate in Centre-State relations:\n1. While the nominal tax devolution is 41%, the *effective* devolution of gross central tax revenues is around 30% due to cesses.\n2. The Constitution places NO capping limit on the percentage of total taxes that can be collected via cesses and surcharges.\n3. The Finance Commission is constitutionally bound to compensate states for the revenue lost due to Union cesses.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. There is no constitutional mandate for the FC to compensate states for Union cesses, as cesses are legally outside the divisible pool. States argue this is unfair, but it is constitutionally valid. Statements 1 and 2 correctly highlight the root of the fiscal friction.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"In the context of the Finance Commission, what does 'Horizontal Imbalance' mean?\n1. The disparity between the revenue-raising powers of the Centre and the States.\n2. The fiscal incapacities among different states due to historical, geographical, and demographic variations.\n3. The mismatch between the budget estimates and revised estimates of a state.\nWhich is correct?","options":["2 only","1 only","3 only","1 and 2 only"],"correct_answer":"2 only","explanation":"Horizontal imbalance refers to the disparities IN the state tier—between various individual states (e.g., wealthy industrial states vs poorer or hilly states). Vertical imbalance refers to Centre vs States. Only statement 2 is correct.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"Consider the impact of the 10th Finance Commission's 'Alternative Scheme of Devolution':\n1. It required states to surrender their right to levy sales tax.\n2. It pooled all Central taxes, meaning states now share the buoyancy of taxes like Corporation Tax and Customs Duties.\n3. It was implemented via the 80th Constitutional Amendment Act.\nWhich are correct?","options":["2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"2 and 3 only","explanation":"Statement 1 is incorrect. Surrendering sales tax autonomy happened later with GST (101st Amendment). The 80th Amendment simply pooled existing central taxes (Customs, Corporation, etc., previously exclusively Union) into the divisible pool with Income and Excise. Statements 2 and 3 are correct.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"Regarding the procedural working of the Finance Commission:\n1. It issues memorandums to all states, calling for estimates of receipts and expenditures.\n2. It conducts on-ground visits to states to consult with CMs, civil society, and local bodies.\n3. Its final report is debated in the Lok Sabha before the President accepts it.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. The report is submitted to the President, and the Cabinet considers it and accepts it (generating the Action Taken Report). It is laid before Parliament AFTER executive acceptance; it does not require a Lok Sabha debate to be accepted. Statements 1 and 2 describe its standard working methodology.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
    {"text":"The Supreme Court in its fiscal federalism jurisprudence has observed that while the Finance Commission's recommendations aren't legally binding:\n1. Non-acceptance by the Union without valid reasons would violate the spirit of cooperative federalism.\n2. Article 281 ensures political consequences for non-acceptance, thus establishing a 'constitutional convention' of acceptance.\n3. State governments can sue the Union in SC under Article 131 if a specific FC recommendation is rejected.\nWhich are correct?","options":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"correct_answer":"1 and 2 only","explanation":"Statement 3 is incorrect. Rejecting an advisory recommendation does not create an actionable legal dispute under Article 131. However, the convention is strong (Statements 1 and 2), and deviations (like keeping Sectoral grants under consideration) face intense parliamentary and political pressure.","chapter_number":51,"level":3},
]
